1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,467
That sentence is typed into
pull requests every day.

2
00:00:07,507 --> 00:00:11,632
A pull request is a proposal on GitHub
to merge one branch into another.

3
00:00:11,672 --> 00:00:16,432
A reviewer approved three commits on Tuesday.
On Wednesday the author force-pushes,

4
00:00:16,472 --> 00:00:19,729
replacing those commits on the
server, with that comment.

5
00:00:19,769 --> 00:00:23,083
There are still three commits,
with the same three titles.

6
00:00:23,423 --> 00:00:26,825
The CTO's question: "The reviewer
approved the first version.

7
00:00:26,865 --> 00:00:29,363
What exactly is different
in the second one?"

8
00:00:29,703 --> 00:00:32,722
The reviewer has three
options. Trust the sentence.

9
00:00:32,762 --> 00:00:37,579
Review everything again. Or run one command
that compares the old series with the

10
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:41,837
new one, commit by commit, and prints
only what changed in the patches.

11
00:00:41,877 --> 00:00:46,814
In today's demo, that command finds a
constant that was 20 when it was approved and

12
00:00:46,854 --> 00:00:47,413
is 12 now.

13
00:00:49,253 --> 00:00:52,959
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

14
00:00:52,999 --> 00:00:57,319
Two tools today, one small and one
that deserves more use than it gets.

15
00:00:57,659 --> 00:01:01,820
The small one is git pull --rebase.
You have both halves already.

16
00:01:01,860 --> 00:01:07,249
Fetch, from the remotes module, downloads
what the server has and moves no branch of

17
00:01:07,289 --> 00:01:12,613
yours. Rebase, from this module, copies
your commits onto a new starting point, and

18
00:01:12,653 --> 00:01:17,977
every copy gets a new ID. You'll see them
run together, and see why this particular

19
00:01:18,017 --> 00:01:20,110
rewriting of history is harmless.

20
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:25,178
The second is git range-diff. Every earlier
video in this module ended with some

21
00:01:25,218 --> 00:01:29,470
version of "check what the rebase
did". This is the tool for that check.

22
00:01:29,510 --> 00:01:34,358
It answers the question "how do these two
versions of a series of commits differ",

23
00:01:34,398 --> 00:01:39,305
which no ordinary diff answers. You'll read
its four markers, see why comparing the

24
00:01:39,345 --> 00:01:44,014
two tips directly is not the same thing,
and see how a reviewer runs it without

25
00:01:44,054 --> 00:01:48,782
access to the author's clone. You'll also
meet its one limit, so that it doesn't

26
00:01:48,822 --> 00:01:49,497
mislead you.

27
00:01:51,337 --> 00:01:52,916
Here's what you'll walk away with.

28
00:01:53,256 --> 00:01:54,668
After this video you can:

29
00:01:55,008 --> 00:02:00,224
Explain git pull --rebase as fetch
followed by a rebase onto the upstream.

30
00:02:00,564 --> 00:02:03,763
Compare the old and the new version
of a branch with git range-diff.

31
00:02:04,103 --> 00:02:08,445
Read the =, !, <, and > markers.

32
00:02:08,785 --> 00:02:11,596
Show a reviewer what changed
in a force-pushed branch.

33
00:02:13,436 --> 00:02:18,853
Pull with rebase. In one sentence: git pull
--rebase 🟡 CAUTION is git fetch followed

34
00:02:18,893 --> 00:02:23,401
by a rebase of your unpushed commits
onto the fetched upstream branch.

35
00:02:23,741 --> 00:02:28,035
On screen: your main has one commit of its
own, and origin/main, your record of the

36
00:02:28,075 --> 00:02:30,802
upstream branch on the server,
has one that you lack.

37
00:02:31,142 --> 00:02:36,030
Your local commits end up on top of the
upstream's, and no merge commit is written.

38
00:02:36,070 --> 00:02:38,287
pull.rebase=true makes it the default.

39
00:02:38,627 --> 00:02:41,827
The textbook is plain about what
this is: history rewriting.

40
00:02:42,167 --> 00:02:46,650
And it's harmless here for one reason:
the only commits rewritten are ones that

41
00:02:46,690 --> 00:02:51,459
exist nowhere but in your clone. That's the
private-or-shared question from the undo

42
00:02:51,499 --> 00:02:53,005
module, answered "private".

43
00:02:53,345 --> 00:02:58,213
One more fact, which you met from the
receiving end in the video on forced pushes.

44
00:02:58,253 --> 00:03:03,240
When the upstream branch itself was rewritten,
git pull --rebase consults the reflog

45
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:07,849
of the remote-tracking branch, the local
list of its past positions, to avoid

46
00:03:07,889 --> 00:03:12,877
replaying commits that upstream discarded.
The next two videos show that logic doing

47
00:03:12,917 --> 00:03:14,074
good and doing harm.

48
00:03:14,414 --> 00:03:19,464
Range-diff. In one sentence: git range-diff
🟢 SAFE compares two versions of a series

49
00:03:19,504 --> 00:03:24,494
of commits: it pairs each old commit with
its new counterpart and shows how the two

50
00:03:24,534 --> 00:03:28,979
patches differ. A patch is the change
one commit makes, written as a diff.

51
00:03:29,319 --> 00:03:34,048
On screen, today's example: three old
commits, which ORIG_HEAD still names, and

52
00:03:34,088 --> 00:03:36,100
their three copies on feat/rerank.

53
00:03:36,440 --> 00:03:41,299
Precisely. The most convenient spelling
is git range-diff <base> <old tip> <new

54
00:03:41,339 --> 00:03:46,198
tip>. Commits are paired by similarity
of their patches, not by position or ID.

55
00:03:46,238 --> 00:03:49,360
Between the two columns
stands one of four markers.

56
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:54,228
For a ! pair Git prints a diff of the
two diffs. Each line has two markers.

57
00:03:54,268 --> 00:03:59,100
The outer marker says whether a line
belongs to the old patch, minus, or the new

58
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:02,084
patch, plus. The inner
marker is the patch's own.

59
00:04:02,424 --> 00:04:07,335
When is range-diff not enough? Its pairing
is a heuristic, so a small patch whose

60
00:04:07,375 --> 00:04:10,574
context changed may be
reported as removed and added.

61
00:04:10,614 --> 00:04:15,646
And its output is for people: the manual
warns that the format may change, so don't

62
00:04:15,686 --> 00:04:16,196
parse it.

63
00:04:23,036 --> 00:04:27,815
An ordinary git diff between the old tip
and the new tip compares two trees, two

64
00:04:27,855 --> 00:04:32,514
full snapshots of the files. After a
rebase onto a moved main, those two trees

65
00:04:32,554 --> 00:04:37,453
differ by everything that arrived from
upstream, plus whatever the author changed.

66
00:04:37,493 --> 00:04:39,140
The author's part is buried.

67
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:44,362
A range-diff ignores the trees. It lays the
old patches next to the new patches and

68
00:04:44,402 --> 00:04:49,285
asks, for each pair: is this the same change?
Think of two editions of a contract's

69
00:04:49,325 --> 00:04:54,268
amendments. You don't compare the two final
contracts, which differ because the base

70
00:04:54,308 --> 00:04:59,131
contract changed. You compare amendment 2
of the first edition with amendment 2 of

71
00:04:59,171 --> 00:04:59,784
the second.

72
00:05:00,124 --> 00:05:05,025
Where does the picture break? Amendments
are numbered, and commits are not paired by

73
00:05:05,065 --> 00:05:09,613
number. Git pairs by similarity. A
reordered series is still paired correctly.

74
00:05:09,653 --> 00:05:13,084
A tiny patch with changed context
may not be paired at all.

75
00:05:13,124 --> 00:05:14,731
That's the limit you'll see.

76
00:05:16,571 --> 00:05:21,505
All six IDs differ, as they must after a
rebase. Only the middle line says that

77
00:05:21,545 --> 00:05:22,639
something changed.

78
00:05:24,479 --> 00:05:26,225
Part 1: pull with rebase.

79
00:05:26,565 --> 00:05:32,290
Ahead 1, behind 1. A plain git pull refuses
here until you choose how to reconcile,

80
00:05:32,330 --> 00:05:34,026
as in the remotes module.

81
00:05:34,366 --> 00:05:38,831
Choosing rebase. Predict the
graph. Quick quiz, two options.

82
00:05:38,871 --> 00:05:42,810
Your commit is 139c7e6.
Option one: it keeps that ID.

83
00:05:42,850 --> 00:05:46,339
Option two: it gets a new
one. Say it out loud.

84
00:05:51,179 --> 00:05:57,242
A straight line, and option two. Your commit
139c7e6 was replayed as ad181ff on top

85
00:05:57,282 --> 00:06:03,344
of Asha's. The reflog labels the steps pull
--rebase: start, pick, finish, the same

86
00:06:03,384 --> 00:06:05,256
three verbs as any rebase.

87
00:06:05,596 --> 00:06:09,805
And the push is a fast-forward for the
server: its branch only moves ahead.

88
00:06:10,145 --> 00:06:13,526
No force. The rewritten commit
had never left your clone.

89
00:06:13,866 --> 00:06:18,935
Part 2: range-diff after a
conflict. labs/run ch09/range-diff.

90
00:06:18,975 --> 00:06:24,621
A rebase with one conflict: main
says TOP_K = 8, your commit says 20.

91
00:06:24,661 --> 00:06:29,730
You settle on 12 in the editor,
a value that neither side had.

92
00:06:30,070 --> 00:06:34,218
The rebase finished. Three
commits, same titles. Now:

93
00:06:34,558 --> 00:06:39,160
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds:
write one marker for each of the three

94
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:40,320
commits. I'll wait.

95
00:06:45,160 --> 00:06:48,732
Commits 1 and 3: equals
sign, unchanged as patches.

96
00:06:48,772 --> 00:06:53,264
Commit 2: exclamation mark. Read
the four marked lines in pairs.

97
00:06:53,304 --> 00:06:58,930
Lines whose outer marker is a minus belong
to the old patch: it turned TOP_K = 5

98
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:04,879
into 20. Lines whose outer marker is a plus
belong to the new patch: it turns 8 into

99
00:07:04,919 --> 00:07:10,473
12. Same title, different change. If the
double markers look like noise, that's

100
00:07:10,513 --> 00:07:12,810
normal: read the outer one first.

101
00:07:13,150 --> 00:07:18,212
This is the answer to the CTO's question
from the opening: 20 when approved, 12 now.

102
00:07:18,252 --> 00:07:21,006
And it's invisible in a
list of commit titles.

103
00:07:21,346 --> 00:07:26,935
Two other spellings. The reflog form,
feat/rerank@{1}, where the branch was one move

104
00:07:26,975 --> 00:07:32,296
ago, needs no ORIG_HEAD. The three-dot
form has no base, so the commit that came

105
00:07:32,336 --> 00:07:35,982
from main is reported as new,
with a greater-than sign.

106
00:07:36,322 --> 00:07:38,500
Why not compare the two tips directly?

107
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:43,643
That's a comparison of two snapshots. Here
it happens to show the resolution, 20

108
00:07:43,683 --> 00:07:48,728
against 12. On a real branch it also contains
everything that arrived from upstream.

109
00:07:48,768 --> 00:07:51,331
Range-diff compares the
commits themselves.

110
00:07:51,671 --> 00:07:55,526
Part 3: the reviewer's side.
labs/run ch09/range-diff-review.

111
00:07:55,566 --> 00:08:00,827
The reviewer doesn't need your ORIG_HEAD.
After a fetch, the previous tip is in the

112
00:08:00,867 --> 00:08:04,147
reflog of the reviewer's
own remote-tracking branch.

113
00:08:04,487 --> 00:08:08,002
The fetch prints "(forced
update)" for feat/ingest.

114
00:08:08,042 --> 00:08:13,857
origin/feat/ingest@{1} is the tip before
that fetch, dc5df93, and origin/feat/ingest

115
00:08:13,897 --> 00:08:14,902
is the tip now.

116
00:08:15,242 --> 00:08:19,809
Two equals lines: nothing changed except
the base, and the earlier review still

117
00:08:19,849 --> 00:08:20,218
stands.

118
00:08:20,558 --> 00:08:25,519
In this case "rebased, no functional change"
was true, and now it's evidence instead

119
00:08:25,559 --> 00:08:26,174
of a claim.

120
00:08:26,514 --> 00:08:30,698
Part 4: a limit to know. labs/run
ch09/range-diff-context.

121
00:08:30,738 --> 00:08:36,087
Run the tool on the clean rebase of
video 52, in which nothing was edited.

122
00:08:36,127 --> 00:08:40,020
Would you predict three equals
signs? Say it out loud.

123
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:49,957
Not quite. The third commit is reported as
removed and added: a less-than line and a

124
00:08:49,997 --> 00:08:54,849
greater-than line under the same subject.
Its patch is one added line with three

125
00:08:54,889 --> 00:08:59,741
lines of context, and one of those context
lines changed underneath it: main had

126
00:08:59,781 --> 00:09:04,817
moved the model from small-v1 to small-v2.
For so small a patch that is too large a

127
00:09:04,857 --> 00:09:07,997
difference, and range-diff
declines to pair the two.

128
00:09:08,037 --> 00:09:10,504
Raise the tolerance and the pair appears.

129
00:09:10,844 --> 00:09:16,274
With --creation-factor=90 the pair is
found, marked with an exclamation mark, and

130
00:09:16,314 --> 00:09:18,976
its only difference is the context line.

131
00:09:19,316 --> 00:09:24,198
So a less-than and a greater-than under
one subject are a prompt to look closer.

132
00:09:24,238 --> 00:09:29,182
A lost commit has a less-than and no
partner. You saw that one in the last video,

133
00:09:29,222 --> 00:09:30,535
after the --ours trap.

134
00:09:32,375 --> 00:09:34,023
Five mistakes to watch for.

135
00:09:34,363 --> 00:09:37,618
Reviewing a force-pushed
branch by its commit titles.

136
00:09:37,658 --> 00:09:42,467
Root cause: a rebase keeps messages by
default, so a patch that changed during

137
00:09:42,507 --> 00:09:45,140
conflict resolution
carries the same title.

138
00:09:45,480 --> 00:09:49,339
Using git diff <old tip> <new tip>
to see what a rebase changed.

139
00:09:49,379 --> 00:09:54,029
Root cause: that compares two snapshots,
which also differ by everything that

140
00:09:54,069 --> 00:09:55,613
arrived from the new base.

141
00:09:55,953 --> 00:09:58,857
Believing the reviewer needs
the author's ORIG_HEAD.

142
00:09:58,897 --> 00:10:03,442
Root cause: the reviewer's fetch recorded
the previous tip in the reflog of their

143
00:10:03,482 --> 00:10:05,480
own remote-tracking branch, as @{1}.

144
00:10:05,820 --> 00:10:10,342
Reading <, then >, under one
subject as a lost and a new commit.

145
00:10:10,382 --> 00:10:16,116
Root cause: pairing is a similarity
heuristic; a small patch with changed context

146
00:10:16,156 --> 00:10:19,965
may not be paired until the
creation factor is raised.

147
00:10:20,305 --> 00:10:23,847
Treating git pull --rebase as
free of risk on any branch.

148
00:10:23,887 --> 00:10:29,126
Root cause: it rewrites your unpushed commits,
which is harmless only because nobody

149
00:10:29,166 --> 00:10:34,216
else has them; on a branch whose upstream
was rewritten, its fork-point logic can

150
00:10:34,256 --> 00:10:35,033
drop commits.

151
00:10:36,873 --> 00:10:41,344
Now, out of the lab. Suppose a team that
reviews prompt and evaluation changes

152
00:10:41,384 --> 00:10:44,293
adopts two rules from this
section of the textbook.

153
00:10:44,633 --> 00:10:49,462
For authors: run range-diff after every
rebase that stopped, before you push, and

154
00:10:49,502 --> 00:10:54,331
paste the output into the pull request
when you force-push a branch under review.

155
00:10:54,371 --> 00:10:57,758
The comment is no longer
"rebased, no functional change".

156
00:10:57,798 --> 00:11:02,747
It's three lines with equals signs, or it's
an exclamation mark with an explanation

157
00:11:02,787 --> 00:11:03,408
next to it.

158
00:11:03,748 --> 00:11:08,097
For reviewers: after a fetch that prints
"(forced update)" for a branch you had

159
00:11:08,137 --> 00:11:12,597
approved, run git range-diff origin/main
"origin/<branch>@{1}" origin/<branch> in

160
00:11:12,637 --> 00:11:16,763
your own clone. It takes seconds, and
it doesn't depend on trusting anyone.

161
00:11:17,103 --> 00:11:20,638
One caution the textbook attaches:
the format is for people.

162
00:11:20,678 --> 00:11:24,629
The manual warns that it may change,
so don't parse it in a script.

163
00:11:26,469 --> 00:11:33,299
Your turn. Do Exercise 9.4, Level 2,
"the old commits and the new ones", in

164
00:11:33,339 --> 00:11:36,322
exercises/m06-m10-integration.md.

165
00:11:36,662 --> 00:11:38,440
Predict before you run range-diff:

166
00:11:38,780 --> 00:11:42,983
The three arguments: which commit is the
base, which is the old tip, which is the

167
00:11:43,023 --> 00:11:45,289
new tip, and where you
get the old tip from.

168
00:11:45,629 --> 00:11:47,590
One marker per commit of the series.

169
00:11:47,930 --> 00:11:52,535
For any commit you expect to be marked
with an exclamation mark: the lines of the

170
00:11:52,575 --> 00:11:55,002
old patch and of the
new patch that differ.

171
00:11:55,342 --> 00:12:00,924
The challenge is Exercise 9.10, Level
5, ""rebased onto main, no functional

172
00:12:00,964 --> 00:12:02,948
change"", in the same file.

173
00:12:04,788 --> 00:12:10,063
Q149: "The reviewer approved three commits;
after your rebase there are still three

174
00:12:10,103 --> 00:12:15,379
with the same titles. How do you show what
changed, and how does the reviewer do it

175
00:12:15,419 --> 00:12:17,300
without access to your clone?"

176
00:12:19,640 --> 00:12:24,340
Answer out loud first. A strong answer
names the tool and, more importantly, says

177
00:12:24,380 --> 00:12:28,260
what it compares and why a comparison
of the two tips would not do.

178
00:12:28,300 --> 00:12:33,175
It gives the author's command with the three
arguments in their roles and says where

179
00:12:33,215 --> 00:12:34,520
the old tip comes from.

180
00:12:34,860 --> 00:12:38,823
Then it gives the reviewer's command
and says where, in the reviewer's own

181
00:12:38,863 --> 00:12:43,042
repository, the old tip is recorded and
what had to happen for it to be there.

182
00:12:43,082 --> 00:12:47,423
It ends with how to read the result,
including the case that needs a second look.

183
00:12:49,263 --> 00:12:53,390
Let's land this. You should now be
able to say, in your own words:

184
00:12:53,730 --> 00:12:58,819
git pull --rebase is a fetch followed by
a rebase of my unpushed commits onto the

185
00:12:58,859 --> 00:13:04,138
fetched upstream. It rewrites only commits
that exist nowhere else, so the following

186
00:13:04,178 --> 00:13:05,594
push is a fast-forward.

187
00:13:05,934 --> 00:13:10,617
git range-diff <base> <old tip> <new tip>
pairs the commits of two versions of a

188
00:13:10,657 --> 00:13:13,864
series by similarity and shows
a diff of their patches.

189
00:13:13,904 --> 00:13:18,646
An equals sign means the same patch, an
exclamation mark a changed patch, and the

190
00:13:18,686 --> 00:13:23,428
angle brackets a commit present on one
side only. A reviewer finds the old tip in

191
00:13:23,468 --> 00:13:27,088
the reflog of their own
remote-tracking branch after fetching.

192
00:13:27,128 --> 00:13:32,048
Pairing is a heuristic, so a removed-and-added
pair under one subject needs a closer

193
00:13:32,088 --> 00:13:32,343
look.

194
00:13:34,183 --> 00:13:38,434
Read sections 9.13 and 9.14 of Chapter 9.

195
00:13:38,774 --> 00:13:43,507
Today you turned "rebased, no functional
change" from a claim into evidence.

196
00:13:43,547 --> 00:13:48,091
Run range-diff after your next rebase,
before you push, even a clean one.

197
00:13:48,131 --> 00:13:52,927
Next time: rebasing a branch that other
people use, and recovering from a bad

198
00:13:52,967 --> 00:13:56,695
rebase. Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

199
00:13:56,735 --> 00:13:58,202
See you in the next one.
